# P09.36 — Bare-metal 32-bit ARM (Cortex-M / STM32) support.
#
# Documentation-only patch. No source changes.
#
# # The investigation
#
# Opened as "what do we need to add to support STM32-class
# targets?" The answer surprised me: nothing on the compiler
# side. After P09.35's cross-compilation fix, the existing
# Itanium-ABI machinery was already target-aware enough to
# emit correct ARM32 code. The only integration question is
# how to link the output on a bare-metal target that doesn't
# ship libc++abi.
#
# # The probe
#
# `/tmp/p09-39-arm32/` — a minimum-viable `#[no_std]`
# staticlib with a single polymorphic `#[repr(cpp)]` class:
#
# ```rust
# #![no_std]
# #![feature(rustc_attrs)]
#
# pub class Widget {
#     v: i32,
#     #[constructor]  pub fn new(v: i32) -> Self { Widget { v } }
#     #[cpp_virtual]  pub fn foo(&self) -> i32 { self.v + 100 }
# }
# ```
#
# Build:
#
# ```
# RUSTC=/path/to/stage1/bin/rustc RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP=1 \
# cargo +nightly build --target thumbv7em-none-eabihf \
#   -Zbuild-std=core,compiler_builtins
# ```
#
# (Cargo-version quirk: the stage-1 cargo is older than the
# `library/std/Cargo.toml`'s edition-2024 requirement, so we
# drive the build with a nightly cargo and override `RUSTC`
# to the fork's stage-1 binary.)
#
# # What came out
#
# LLVM IR:
#
# - `target triple = "thumbv7em-unknown-none-eabihf"` ✓
# - `target datalayout = "...p:32:32..."` ✓ 32-bit pointers
# - Ctor: `define void @_ZN6WidgetC1Ei(ptr sret([8 x i8])
#   align 4 %_0, i32 %v)` — Itanium-style void-return sret.
# - Vtable: `{ i32, ptr, ptr }` = offset_to_top + RTTI +
#   1 fn pointer, 4-byte-pointer-sized slots.
# - Vptr GEP: `getelementptr inbounds (i8, ptr @_ZTV6Widget,
#   i32 8)` — 8-byte address-point offset (2 × 4-byte ptrs).
# - All symbols mangled to Itanium spec.
#
# Cross-compiler verification via `arm-none-eabi-g++ 15.2.0`
# (Homebrew):
#
# - C++ `Widget::Widget(int32_t)` mangles to `_ZN6WidgetC1Ei`,
#   identical to Rust.
# - Call site disassembly: `bl _ZN6WidgetC1Ei` with `r0 = this`,
#   `r1 = v`, return value ignored. Matches Rust's sret+void
#   calling convention byte-for-byte.
# - Virtual dispatch: `ldr r3, [obj, #0]; ldr r3, [r3, #0];
#   blx r3` — loads vptr from object offset 0 (our vtable
#   address point), reads first fn pointer, calls. Matches
#   our emission exactly.
#
# # The only link-time knob
#
# `_ZTVN10__cxxabiv117__class_type_infoE` appears as an
# undefined reference in the Rust static archive (the
# typeinfo struct's RTTI-base vtable pointer). On hosted
# systems this is provided by libc++abi; on bare-metal ARM
# users don't normally link it.
#
# The workaround is a 3-line C stub:
#
# ```c
# __attribute__((weak))
# void* _ZTVN10__cxxabiv117__class_type_infoE[4] = { 0 };
# ```
#
# This satisfies the linker; runtime RTTI (`typeid`,
# `dynamic_cast`) requires real libc++abi but virtual
# dispatch through the vtable does NOT consult typeinfo, so
# all `#[cpp_virtual]` calls work with just the stub.
# Alternative: link against `libsupc++-nano` shipped with
# `arm-none-eabi-gcc`.
#
# # Link verification
#
# Partial-relocatable link:
#
# ```
# arm-none-eabi-g++ -mcpu=cortex-m4 -mfpu=fpv4-sp-d16 \
#   -mfloat-abi=hard -mthumb -c caller.cpp -o caller.o
# arm-none-eabi-gcc ... -c rtti_stub.c -o rtti_stub.o
# arm-none-eabi-ld -r caller.o rtti_stub.o \
#   --whole-archive libp09_39_arm32.a -o probe.o
# ```
#
# Result: `probe.o: ELF 32-bit LSB relocatable, ARM, EABI5
# version 1`. Zero unresolved symbols.
#
# # Other ARM Cortex-M targets
#
# The fork's C++ ABI paths don't touch target-specific
# instruction selection, so the recipe extends to any
# Itanium-ABI 32-bit ARM variant:
#
# - `thumbv7m-none-eabi` — Cortex-M3.
# - `thumbv7em-none-eabi` — Cortex-M4 (no FPU).
# - `thumbv7em-none-eabihf` — Cortex-M4F (tested).
# - `thumbv8m.base-none-eabi` — Cortex-M23.
# - `thumbv8m.main-none-eabihf` — Cortex-M33F.
#
# If a specific triple produces different mangling vs the
# toolchain's own C++ compiler, file an issue with the exact
# ABI fact that mismatches.
#
# # What changed in v1
#
# - `getting-started.html` §16 documents the build recipe,
#   RTTI stub, and known ABI facts.
# - Feature matrix adds "Bare-metal ARM Cortex-M" as
#   supported, with the libc++abi stub requirement noted.
# - Targets list expanded to include
#   `thumbv7em-*`, `thumbv7m-*`, `thumbv8m.*`.
#
# No compiler or workspace code changes. The probe at
# `/tmp/p09-39-arm32/` serves as the integration test.
#
# # Takeaway saved to memory
#
# Before estimating a "new target support" effort, build the
# minimum-viable probe first. What looked like a 2-4 day
# project (ARM32 ABI overlay) turned out to be zero-code
# after P09.35 made `CxxTarget` target-aware. The v1 fork
# was already more portable than the earlier documentation
# admitted.
